          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines maybe six times a month.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The red-flag rule table did not match this description. That is not a clearance. If any of the warning signs below appear, get medical help promptly.\n\n**Red flags that make a headache urgent** (from the migraine condition summary, Consilium migraine reference): seek urgent assessment if a headache:\n- Comes on suddenly and reaches full intensity within about a minute.\n- Comes with fever and a stiff neck.\n- Follows a head injury.\n- Comes with new weakness, numbness, confusion, vision loss, or trouble speaking that does not go away.\n- Starts after age 50 as a new kind of headache.\n- Gets steadily worse over days or weeks.\n\nIf you have any of these, or your headache feels unlike your usual pattern, call emergency services or seek care now.\n\n**Does six a month need a doctor visit?** The sources I could retrieve do not give a frequency number that automatically means you need review. The migraine guideline note says preventive treatment is considered when attacks are frequent or disabling. So the more useful question is whether your migraines are disrupting your life or changing over time. A clinician can judge that. Also, the guideline warns that taking acute headache medicine too often can keep headaches going. It sets limits of fewer than 10 treatment days a month for triptans, ergots, opioids and combination pain relievers, and fewer than 15 days a month for simple pain relievers (migraine guideline note, medication-overuse section). Going over these limits is worth raising with a clinician.\n\n**Questions that would clarify the pattern:**\n- How long does each attack last, and how severe is it?\n- How many days a month do you take headache medicine, and which kind?\n- Has the pattern changed recently, or has it been steady?\n- Do attacks stop your work, school, or daily activities?\n- Do you notice triggers, such as poor sleep, skipped meals, or stress?\n- Do any of the red-flag features above ever occur with your attacks?\n\nI could not run one more search to check for additional guidance, so this answer is limited to the passages above. A clinician can review your pattern in person.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
